Retry a clean-up batch that conflicts with a concurrent write

Barbican 23.0.0, and 22.0.0 with the backport of upstream commit
a28ddb94c3, runs `barbican-manage db clean` in batches: six copies of
one `while True` loop in barbican/model/clean.py select up to 10,000
ids with a plain read, change those rows by primary key and commit.
Three gaps remain:

- No batch is retried. A batch that loses against a concurrent API
  write fails the run.
- `_soft_delete_expired_secrets` is still one `UPDATE` that reads all
  of `secrets` (no index covers `deleted` or `expiration`) and holds
  its locks until the next batch commits, or until the end of the run
  when no batch follows.
- A loop that finds no id leaves its transaction open, so the next
  table is read from the old snapshot, and a row written since then
  fails the batch with MariaDB error 1020 ("Record has changed since
  last read", innodb_snapshot_isolation=ON under REPEATABLE READ).

Measured on 2026-11-13 against MariaDB 12.3.3 (one node) with eight
connections running the statements the API runs, the published 2026.2
image failed 4 of 6 runs with 30,000 soft-deleted secrets and their
metadata in the retention window (1020 on `secret_user_metadata` and on
`secrets`), and 1 of 8 runs with `--min-days 0` (1020 on `orders`).
oslo.db (18.0.0 for 2026.1, 18.1.1 for 2026.2) already classifies the
errors: `exc_filters` raises `DBDeadlock` for MySQL errors 1213 and
1205, and its subclass `DBConsistencyError` for 1020. The clean-up
catches neither.

The patch moves every loop onto two helpers. `_run_batch` reads one
batch of ids, changes the rows in statements of at most 500 ids
(`_IDS_PER_STATEMENT`) and commits; on `DBDeadlock` it rolls
the session back, which ends the transaction and its snapshot, logs a
WARNING naming the table, and re-raises, so that
`oslo_db.api.wrap_db_retry(retry_on_deadlock=True)` waits and runs the
batch again, up to `CLEANUP_BATCH_MAX_RETRIES = 10` times. Any other
exception passes through on its first occurrence. `_run_batches` loops
until the id query finds nothing and sums the counts.
`_soft_delete_expired_secrets` becomes a batched loop of its own:
ids of live secrets that expired, an `UPDATE` by id, one commit per
batch; each batch shrinks the candidate set, which ends the loop.

A statement names at most 500 ids because the retry alone does not
reach a batch whose statement scans the table. MariaDB 12.3.3 runs
`DELETE FROM secrets WHERE id IN (...)` with the 10,000 ids of a full
batch as a full scan of a 50,000-row table, and with 9,000 ids or fewer
by primary key. Under REPEATABLE READ the scan reads every row with a
lock, so it fails with 1020 on any row written after the batch read its
ids, and each retry meets the same race. Under the four writers of the
reproduction in issue C5C3/cobaltcore#1705, 20 of 20 attempts failed
with 10,000 ids per statement and 0 of 20 with 500, and the retry
without the split failed 4 of 5 runs after ten retries of one batch.
With 500 ids a statement stays on the primary key on tables of 5,000
rows and more, and it reads only the rows of its batch. The batch, and
with it the commit, stays at 10,000 rows.

`_run_batch` sets the intervals of wrap_db_retry to the defaults of
oslo.db 18, so that an oslo.db bump cannot change them: the first wait
is 1 s, each later one a random time below a bound that doubles from
2 s and is capped at 10 s, so ten retries of one batch wait 75 s at
most, against the 3,600 s activeDeadlineSeconds of the
barbican-operator's clean-up Job. When the retries of a batch run out,
the `DBDeadlock` reaches `clean_command`, which logs, rolls back the
open batch and re-raises; the process exits non-zero, the Job retries
the pod, and the next attempt continues behind the committed batches.

The helpers retry per batch rather than per public function: a retry
decorator on `cleanup_softdeletes` and its siblings would restart a
table from its first batch, count the rows of the last attempt only,
and spend the ten retries per table instead of per batch.
`_run_batch` neither rolls back before its first attempt nor commits an
empty batch: the upstream tests build their fixtures uncommitted in
the session the clean-up uses, and two of them pin "no commit for the
empty trailing query". The open snapshot that leaves can fail the
first attempt of the next batch, and the retry then reads a new one.

Without concurrent writers the patched code takes as long as the
unpatched 23.0.0 code: 1.6 to 1.7 s for 90,000 soft-deleted secrets
in both.

Tests: no upstream test moves. The commit counts per table stay the
same, `test_cleanup_softdeletes_partial_progress_is_durable` raises a
`RuntimeError`, which is not retried, and
`test_db_cleanup_raise_integrity_error` expects a `DBReferenceError`,
which is not a `DBDeadlock`. The patch adds eight tests to
barbican/tests/cmd/test_db_cleanup.py: a batch retried after
`DBDeadlock` and after `DBConsistencyError`, committed batches kept
across a retry, `DBDeadlock` raised after the last retry with the rows
of that batch in place, the expired-secrets pass committing per batch,
no commit when nothing expired, one batch split over several
statements and committed once, and a batch whose second statement
raises `DBConsistencyError`, rolled back and run again with every row
counted once. All but the nothing-expired test fail without the
patch.

Proof: tests/container-images/verify_barbican.sh (Test 9) asserts in
the built image that a clean-up batch whose commit raises `DBDeadlock`
is run again and committed.

Applies to barbican 22.0.0 (2026.1), on top of 0001, the backport of
the batched clean-up.

Upstream status: not yet proposed.

